# AssettoServer operations

> **Verified against**: `AssettoServer v0.0.55-pre25` — the source tree at `~/works/AssettoServer`
> (commit `51d8d8a6`), which is what the DriveZone image `drivezone/assettoserver:local` reports as
> `AssettoServer 0.0.55+51d8d8a6e0`. Probed on 2026-09-05: all 15 `extra_cfg.yml` keys cited here
> resolve to properties under `Server/Configuration/Extra/`; 24 of the 25 `server_cfg.ini` keys
> resolve to the sample `Assets/server_cfg.ini` or to an `[IniField]` under
> `Server/Configuration/Kunos/` — `CARS` is a stock acServer key this source never reads; the
> density function quoted below is `Server/Ai/AiBehavior.cs:455-466` at that tag. Not probed: no
> server was started against a client, and the CSP build the DriveZone servers require is not
> recorded on this machine.

Distilled from a production freeroam deployment (DriveZone on SRP/Shutoko). The runtime is
`compujuckel/AssettoServer` — a CSP-aware replacement for stock `acServer` that adds
`extra_cfg.yml`, an AI traffic engine, and a .NET plugin loader. Prescriptive: follow these unless
the project documents a deliberate exception.

## Repository shape — what is (not) versioned

- Versioned: ONLY the `.example` twins (`server_cfg.ini.example`, `entry_list.ini.example`,
  `extra_cfg.yml.example`, `welcome.txt.example`), holding fictional values
  (`PASSWORD=change-me-local-password`) and the safe defaults (`EnableAi: false`,
  `EnablePlugins: []`). The real `server_cfg.ini`/`entry_list.ini`/`extra_cfg.yml`/plugin YAML
  are git-ignored local files — that is where AI traffic and plugins actually get enabled.
  Real secrets and private endpoints never enter git.
- NEVER versioned: `server/content/` (cars/tracks/traffic packs are proprietary) and the runtime
  install itself.
- Before any risky config change, snapshot into `server/cfg/backups/<label>-<timestamp>/`
  (e.g. `traffic-test-before-tatsumi-pa-20260628185132`). Rollback is a copy, not an archaeology
  session.
- The runtime binary is NOT baked into the Docker image: an install script downloads the release
  selected by `ASSETTOSERVER_VERSION` (env) from GitHub into a named volume on first boot —
  `latest` is the observed default; PIN an explicit version before a beta/event so client
  CSP/content expectations can't drift under you. The start script symlinks
  cfg/content/logs/results into the install dir and **fails fast when no cars/tracks are mounted**.

## Config anatomy — the three files must agree

| File | Owns | Hard rules |
|---|---|---|
| `server_cfg.ini` | stock AC: `CARS`, `TRACK`, `CONFIG_TRACK` (layout), `MAX_CLIENTS`, ports | `MAX_CLIENTS` = number of `[CAR_N]` blocks INCLUDING AI slots; `CARS` lists human + traffic models |
| `entry_list.ini` | one `[CAR_N]` block per slot | traffic slots MUST carry `AI=Fixed` — without it AssettoServer treats them as human pickup slots |
| `extra_cfg.yml` | AssettoServer extras: `EnableAi`, `AiParams`, `CSP:`, `EnablePlugins:`, `Logs:` | `EnablePlugins: []` is the safe default (see plugin gate below) |

Dense references: `references/server-cfg-fields.md` (field-by-field table),
`references/entry-list-skeleton.md` (annotated skeleton + indexing rules),
`references/prelaunch-validation.md` (actionable pre-launch checklist).

Quirks that cost real debugging time:

- `WELCOME_MESSAGE` must point to a **file path** (`cfg/welcome.txt`), never inline text.
- Avoid `[PRACTICE] TIME=0` — it can restart the session in a tight loop. Use `TIME=1440`.
- Ports: 9600 tcp+udp (game) and 8081 tcp (HTTP). `curl http://HOST:8081` is the cheapest
  liveness probe a player can run.

## Checksum / CSP — the content contract

Client and server must share **identical** track, layout, car, skin and a compatible CSP, or the
join dies with `checksum mismatch` / `track version mismatch`. Operating rules:

- Pin ONE content baseline (pack version, track id, layout id, car ids, skin ids) and publish it
  to players verbatim. Example baseline: SRP 0.9.3, track `shuto_revival_project_beta`, layout
  `tatsumi_pa`.
- **Never mix beta/`_ptb` variants with the stable pack.** The canonical real-world failure was an
  AI spline from the `_ptb` variant used with the non-ptb track — traffic broke and joins failed.
  Any `_ptb` reference in logs or client installs = wrong content, full stop.
- Canonical log greps when diagnosing:
  `attempting to connect`, `has connected`, `failed checksum`, `checksum mismatch`, `AI Slot`,
  `reached spline`, `warning|error|exception|crash`.
- Symptom → fix table for player-side failures: `references/troubleshooting-checksum.md`.

## AI traffic — enablement discipline

Enabling traffic without the prerequisites produces silent-broken states, so gate it:

1. **Audit before enabling.** The lane files must exist for the configured layout:
   `tracks/<track>/ai/fast_lane.ai` or `fast_lane.aip` (root or layout dir). A
   `models_<layout>_traffic.ini` alone is NOT sufficient — keep traffic disabled until a real
   lane/spline is mounted. Encode this as an audit script that prints a Decision line
   (`traffic may be testable` / `keep traffic disabled`), not as tribal knowledge.
2. Traffic slots in `entry_list.ini` get `AI=Fixed`, a `traffic_*` car model, and stay separate
   from the human car so players can't spawn traffic vehicles.
3. Recount `MAX_CLIENTS` — it includes the AI slots. `EntryCarManager` allocates
   `Math.Min(MaxClients, EntryList.Cars.Count)`, so slots past a stale `MAX_CLIENTS` are dropped
   **silently, with no error**.
4. Size the counts from the formula below — never by copying another server's numbers.

The lane file may live at the track ROOT `ai/` dir and serve all layouts — empty
`<layout>/ai/` dirs are fine; the validator accepts a root `fast_lane.ai|.aip`.

```yaml
EnableAi: true
AiParams:
  MaxPlayerCount: 0            # entry limit, NOT density — see below. Keep 0.
  MaxAiTargetCount: 0          # 0 = server derives it (preferred); see the rules below
  AiPerPlayerTargetCount: 0    # 0 = server derives it (preferred)
  TrafficDensity: 1.0
  NamePrefix: "Traffic"
  HideAiCars: true             # needs CSP >= 0.1.78 client-side
  TwoWayTraffic: false
  WrongWayTraffic: true
  MaxSpeedKph: 85
  RightLaneOffsetKph: 10
  MinAiSafetyDistanceMeters: 20
  MaxAiSafetyDistanceMeters: 70
  SpawnSafetyDistanceToPlayerMeters: 150
```

### How many AI cars actually spawn

Everything about density follows from one function (`Server/Ai/AiBehavior.cs`, tag
`v0.0.55-pre25`). Read it before choosing any value:

```csharp
int playerCount = EntryCars.Count(car => car.Client != null && car.Client.IsConnected);
var aiSlots     = EntryCars.Where(car => car.Client == null && car.AiControlled).ToList();
int targetAiCount = Math.Min(
        playerCount * Math.Min((int)Math.Round(AiPerPlayerTargetCount * TrafficDensity), aiSlots.Count),
        MaxAiTargetCount);
int overbooking = targetAiCount / aiSlots.Count;   // INTEGER division
int rest        = targetAiCount % aiSlots.Count;
for (int i = 0; i < aiSlots.Count; i++)
    aiSlots[i].SetAiOverbooking(i < rest ? overbooking + 1 : overbooking);
```

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| `MaxAiTargetCount` **>= the AI slot count**, or slots die | integer division: target 18 over 25 slots gives `overbooking = 0`, and every slot from index `rest` on gets **zero** states. Those car models never appear in-game — while clients still download and checksum all of them |
| It is a **total budget across all players**, not a CPU ceiling | the outer `Math.Min` caps the sum. Size it `perPlayerTarget * maxConcurrentPlayers`, or density silently halves when the second player joins |
| `AiPerPlayerTargetCount` above the slot count does nothing | the inner `Math.Min` clamps it to `aiSlots.Count` |
| A player never sees more AI cars than the slot count | `EntryCar.cs` emits one `PositionUpdateOut` per **slot** per player (`GetBestStateForPlayer`). Overbooking exists so N players each get their own instance of a slot — it does not thicken traffic for one driver. To raise what a single driver sees, add `AI=Fixed` slots |

**Prefer the server's own arithmetic.** Left at `0`, `ACServerConfiguration` computes
`AiPerPlayerTargetCount = aiSlots` and `MaxAiTargetCount = humanSlots * aiSlots` — correct by
construction for any entry list, and it re-derives when the entry list changes. Hardcode only to go
*below* that, and say why.

`Min/MaxAiSafetyDistanceMeters` are **spawn-placement gates, not following distance** — they appear
only in `IsPositionSafe` / `IsKeepingSafetyDistances`. Car-following is `_minObstacleDistance`
(`AiState.cs`, a random 8–12 m). The draw is uniform in **squared** metres, so it skews high:
raising the Min barely moves the mean gap, while lowering the Max moves it a lot.

### `MaxPlayerCount` is an entry limit, not a traffic knob

It lives under `AiParams`, which is exactly why it gets carried along when someone tunes traffic —
and it is the most damaging value in the block. Above `0`, `AiSlotFilter` reports **every** car as
closed, human slots included, as soon as `ConnectedCars.Count` reaches it; the next player is told
the server is full while the lobby still advertises `MAX_CLIENTS`.

**Keep it `0`.** Players can never take a traffic slot anyway — the same filter rejects `AI=Fixed`
unconditionally — so the ceiling is already the number of slots without an `AI=` key.

Two amplifiers make it near-invisible in testing: admins bypass the filter (whoever operates the
server never reproduces it), and players still on the loading screen count as connected for up to
`PlayerLoadingTimeoutMinutes`, so one stuck client burns capacity for minutes.

### Judging whether the count is too high

Do not guess from FPS. AssettoServer exposes Prometheus metrics on the HTTP port, and the tick loop
is a **single thread** at `CLIENT_SEND_INTERVAL_HZ` (default 20 Hz = a 50 ms budget), so more cores
do not help it. Read:

| Metric | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `assettoserver_acserver_updateasync` p99 | main tick cost — keep well inside the budget |
| `assettoserver_acserver_updateasync_late` | must stay flat; any slope means past capacity |
| `assettoserver_aibehavior_update` p99 | spawn-point finder — the superlinear one |
| `assettoserver_aistatecount` | plateauing below target means spawn-starved, not CPU-bound |

Bandwidth usually binds before CPU: AI near a player is inside `NetworkBubbleDistance` and updates at
full rate, so outbound scales with `players * AI-near-player`. Tunnelling game UDP over a TCP proxy
makes this worse than the byte count suggests — one lost segment stalls every player's position
stream at once, which reads in-game as "the AI cars freeze and jump".

Plugin-side cost is a separate budget: per-tick loops over `EntryCarManager.EntryCars` scale with the
slot count. That belongs to `assettoserver-plugin`.

## Docker / WSL2 orchestration

- One compose service for the server; sidecars (log collector) as **opt-in profiles**
  (`profiles: ["collector"]`) with the log dir mounted **read-only**.
- Reach a host-run backend from containers via
  `extra_hosts: ["host.docker.internal:host-gateway"]`.
- Run as a non-root user (`user: "${UID:-1000}:${GID:-1000}"`); runtime files in a named volume
  (`assettoserver_files`).
- **Link every runtime dir to a mounted path — including the ones the server creates itself.** The
  compiled AI spline cache is the trap: the server writes it to `cache/` **relative to its working
  directory** and `mkdir`s it at runtime, so it never appears in a mount list written by hand. Left
  unlinked it lands on the container's ephemeral layer and the spline is recompiled on every start
  (tens of MB, seconds of boot). A named volume over the install dir hides this locally while
  orchestrators that recreate containers pay it every time — so verify on the deployed target, not
  on the dev box. Two follow-ons: a freshly provisioned volume is root-owned and needs a `chown` to
  the server's UID before first boot, and the cache is derived data — single-replica storage, out of
  backups, safe to delete (it regenerates).
- WSL2 LAN exposure needs Windows-side plumbing — document it next to the compose file:
  `netsh interface portproxy add v4tov4 listenport=9600 connectaddress=$wslIp connectport=9600`
  (repeat for 8081) plus firewall rules for 9600/tcp, 9600/udp, 8081/tcp. The WSL IP changes;
  recompute it (`wsl hostname -I`) after reboots.
- **Static web assets ARE baked into the image** — the inverse of the runtime binary (which lives
  in a named volume, see above). Anything under `server/wwwroot/` served at `/static` (brand
  images, trophies, notification sounds consumed by CSP overlays) is `COPY`'d at build time:
  adding or replacing an asset requires `docker compose build <service>` + `up -d` (recreate).
  **A plain `restart` serves the stale asset** — the classic symptom is "I replaced the file but
  the game still shows/plays the old one".

## Plugin deployment — rite-gated sync

Plugins are built in their own repo (see `assettoserver-plugin`); the operational repo only
receives published artifacts, under a hard gate:

```bash
[[ -f "$RITE_STATUS_FILE" ]] || fail "rite proof missing — run tests/publish/bug-hunter first"
[[ "$RITE_STATUS_FILE" -nt "$PLUGIN_DIR/$PLUGIN_NAME.dll" ]] \
  || fail "rite proof older than the DLL — re-run the bug-hunter after publish"
```

- The sync script REFUSES to copy a DLL without a `plugin-rite-status.json` (written by the plugin
  repo's bug-hunter) newer than the DLL itself. No proof, no deploy — even for "just one line".
- Copy into the runtime named volume via a throwaway container
  (`docker run --rm -v vol:/runtime -v artifacts:/source:ro alpine cp -R ...`); never edit the
  volume by hand.
- `EnablePlugins: []` stays the committed default. Enabling a plugin is a deliberate config change;
  rollback is reverting to the empty list, not deleting files.
- Fix plugin bugs in the plugin repo and re-run its full rite before restarting a real server —
  never patch DLLs or configs directly on the runtime.
- Prove the load BEFORE touching the real config: `prove-drivezone-plugin-load.sh` boots the
  runtime against a TEMPORARY config with the plugin enabled and confirms the load via log,
  leaving the real config untouched.
- The plugin's own config follows the runtime's naming convention:
  `cfg/plugin_<snake_case_plugin_name>_cfg.yml`, with its JSON schema
  (`...cfg.schema.json`) next to it.

## Operational scripts (make each check a script, not a memory)

The deployment keeps ~18 single-purpose bash scripts (`set -euo pipefail`, idempotent), invoked
through the server container: `validate-config.sh`, `audit-traffic-support.sh <layout>`,
`inspect-content.sh`, `diagnose-server.sh`, `check-runtime-logs.sh`, `smoke-server.sh`,
`drivezone-{up,down,status,healthcheck}.sh`, `print-client-requirements.sh` (emits the exact
content list players must install). Troubleshooting docs reference these scripts by name, so the
runbook and the tooling can't drift apart.

